{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And he shall flay the burnt offering.</b> It is a commandment that he flay it entirely and afterward cut it into pieces. And Scripture says \"and he shall flay and cut into pieces\" because it speaks with reference to the owner of the offering, just as it says (Leviticus 1:4) \"and he shall lay his hand and slaughter,\" for flaying and dismemberment are not avodot (sacrificial procedures) and are valid when performed by a zar (non-priest), and therefore it afterward returns and says \"and the sons of Aaron shall place\" (Leviticus 1:7). And likewise, washing the entrails is valid when performed by a zar, and therefore it says \"and its entrails and its legs he shall wash in water\" (Leviticus 1:9) with reference to the owner of the offering, and afterward \"and the priest shall burn.\" And it uses plural language — \"and they shall place,\" \"and they shall arrange\" — because regarding all matters of the priesthood Scripture speaks thus, for the reason that many priests gather in the House of HaShem to perform the burnt offering, and \"in the multitude of people is the king's glory\" (Proverbs 14:28), but it is not a requirement (an impediment if not done by many), for it teaches below (Leviticus 1:12) \"and the priest shall arrange them.\"" "<b>And he shall flay and cut into pieces, and the sons of Aaron shall place — fire.</b> This is not the sequence, for it is fitting that the priests arrange the ma'arachah (wood-pile) and afterward dismember the burnt offering, and such was the sequence of the ma'arachah in the tamid (daily offering) (Yoma 33). And likewise what it says (Leviticus 1:8) \"and the sons of Aaron the priests shall arrange the pieces, the head, and the fat, on the wood that is on the fire\" and afterward \"and the entrails and the legs he shall wash in water\" (Leviticus 1:9) — the command is not to act in this sequence, for the arranging is the burning of the pieces, concerning which it commands below (Leviticus 1:9) \"and the priest shall burn the whole,\" and if so, the sequence is not that he place the pieces and the head and the fat on the fire that is on the altar and afterward wash the entrails and the legs and burn them, but rather first he dismembers and washes and afterward places everything on the fire and burns. But the subject of the verse is that it preceded [with] the tossing of the blood, to teach that it precedes everything, and afterward it commanded the burning of the limbs, and it says that he shall flay and dismember to arrange them on the fire after washing the entrails and the legs, and then he burns everything at once, and this is what it says \"the whole.\" And the intention of Scripture's repetition regarding the limbs, saying \"and they shall arrange wood on the fire and burn,\" is to teach that after he arranges them on the fire he shall not leave them until he kindles the fire among them and they are consumed and the fragrance ascends from them. And likewise, that it preceded flaying and dismemberment to the arrangement of the fire is to teach that in a freewill burnt offering there is no obligation to precede it with a ma'arachah as in the burnt offering of the tamid, concerning which we were commanded (Leviticus 6:5) \"and the priest shall kindle wood upon it morning by morning,\" and it precedes everything [else] on the altar, as is explained in tractate Yoma (Yoma 33)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The final phrase translating מֵאַיִן as the required minimum is inferred from the contrast with the plural forms; the word עִכּוּב literally means 'preclusion/impediment.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:49:39.918520181Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}